## REASONING

The request is to quantify **elevation-resolved** water partitioning across the Bow River headwaters in Alberta, Canada. The single most important feasibility fact dominates this design: **the domain is in Canada, outside CONUS**. NLDAS-2 (the 0.125°, elevation-resolving forcing) is CONUS-only. Outside CONUS the stack falls back to Qian T62 at ~1.9°, which is **not elevation-resolved** — a 2200 m-relief basin (1200–3400 m) would collapse to essentially one forcing cell with no orographic precip/temperature gradient and no lapse rate.

The PRIMARY mechanism the user asks for — how partitioning (ET, runoff, drainage, snow) varies **with elevation** — acts physically through the elevation-dependence of precipitation, temperature, and the rain–snow transition. With uniform Qian forcing, columns placed along an elevation transect would differ only by **soil**, not by climate. Any "elevation gradient" I reported would be a soil artifact mislabelled as an elevation effect — exactly the failure mode the guidance warns against. So the key mechanism is **not representable**.

Validation is also blocked: SNOTEL is US-only (missing here), and ECCC gauges are explicitly "not in stack" — and even if ingested, gauge streamflow needs routing/aggregation the single-column stack lacks. There is no in-domain observation this stack can compare against.

Therefore the verdict is **infeasible as posed**. I will not dress up a soil-only sweep as an elevation study. The honest path is to flag the missing capabilities (elevation-downscaled forcing outside CONUS; an in-stack Canadian observation source) as the development backlog, and note the only thing the stack *could* do here is a soil-controlled conceptual sweep under a single non-elevation-resolved forcing — which does NOT answer the question. I record that as a fallback, not the design.

